# Indian polity and governance: the Constitution, institutions, Panchayati Raj. No office-holders.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    L(1, 2, "Preamble", stmts(CS, [
        "The Preamble has been amended only once, by the 42nd Amendment Act, 1976.",
        "The words 'socialist' and 'secular' were added to the Preamble by the 44th Amendment Act.",
    ]), TWO, 0,
      "Only statement 1 is correct. The 42nd Amendment added 'socialist', 'secular' and 'integrity' to the Preamble, its only amendment so far. The 44th Amendment of 1978 undid several Emergency-era changes but did not touch these words."),
    L(1, 2, "Election Commission", stmts("Consider the following statements about the Election Commission of India:", [
        "The Constitution does not fix the number of Election Commissioners.",
        "It conducts the elections to the offices of the President and the Vice-President.",
    ]), TWO, 2,
      "Both are correct. Article 324 leaves the number of Election Commissioners to the President, and the Commission conducts elections to Parliament, the state legislatures and the offices of President and Vice-President. Panchayat and municipal polls are run by the State Election Commissions."),
    L(1, 2, "Parliament", stmts("Consider the following statements about the Rajya Sabha:", [
        "It is a permanent body and is not subject to dissolution.",
        "About one-third of its members retire every second year.",
        "The Vice-President of India is its ex officio Chairman.",
    ], HOW_ASK), HOW_MANY, 2,
      "All three are correct. Members serve six-year terms with a third retiring every two years, so the House is never dissolved, and Article 89 makes the Vice-President its Chairman."),
    L(1, 2, "Writs", stmts(CP, [
        "Habeas corpus : Produce a detained person before the court",
        "Mandamus : Command a public authority to perform its duty",
        "Quo warranto : Stop a lower court from exceeding its jurisdiction",
    ], PAIRS_ASK), HOW_MANY_PAIRS, 1,
      "Two pairs are correct. Quo warranto asks a person to show by what authority he holds a public office; stopping a lower court from exceeding its jurisdiction is the writ of prohibition."),
    L(1, 1, "Fundamental Duties", stmts("Which of the following are Fundamental Duties listed in Article 51A?", [
        "To protect and improve the natural environment",
        "To pay income tax on time",
        "To safeguard public property and abjure violence",
    ], "Select the correct answer using the code given below."), THREE, 2,
      "Protecting the environment and safeguarding public property are both in Article 51A. Paying taxes is a legal obligation under tax laws but is not one of the Fundamental Duties."),
    S(1, 3, "Anti-defection", "Under the Tenth Schedule, members of a legislature party are protected from disqualification on a merger with another party when the merger is agreed to by at least:",
      ["Two-thirds of the legislature party", "One-third of the legislature party", "Half of the legislature party", "Three-fourths of the legislature party"],
      "Paragraph 4 of the Tenth Schedule protects a merger supported by two-thirds of the legislature party. The older exception for a 'split' by one-third was deleted by the 91st Amendment of 2003."),
    L(1, 2, "Panchayati Raj", stmts("Consider the following statements about the 73rd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1992:", [
        "It added Part IX, 'The Panchayats', to the Constitution.",
        "It reserved not less than one-third of the seats in panchayats for women.",
    ]), TWO, 2,
      "Both are correct. The 73rd Amendment inserted Part IX and the Eleventh Schedule, and Article 243D reserves at least one-third of seats (and of chairperson posts) for women. Many states have raised this to half by their own laws."),
    S(1, 2, "President", "The President's power to promulgate ordinances when Parliament is not in session is given by:",
      ["Article 123", "Article 213", "Article 352", "Article 356"],
      "Article 123 gives the President the ordinance power and Article 213 gives the same power to a Governor. Articles 352 and 356 deal with national emergency and President's rule in a state."),
    L(1, 2, "Schedules", stmts(CP, [
        "Seventh Schedule : Union, State and Concurrent Lists",
        "Eighth Schedule : Languages",
        "Ninth Schedule : Disqualification on grounds of defection",
    ], PAIRS_ASK), HOW_MANY_PAIRS, 1,
      "Two pairs are correct. The Ninth Schedule holds laws protected from challenge, mainly land reform laws, added by the First Amendment in 1951. The anti-defection provisions are in the Tenth Schedule."),
    L(1, 2, "Money Bills", stmts("Consider the following statements about a Money Bill:", [
        "The Rajya Sabha must return it, with or without recommendations, within 14 days.",
        "The Lok Sabha is bound to accept the recommendations of the Rajya Sabha.",
    ]), TWO, 0,
      "Only statement 1 is correct. Under Article 109 the Lok Sabha may accept or reject any or all of the Rajya Sabha's recommendations; if the Rajya Sabha does not return the Bill within 14 days it is deemed passed."),
    L(1, 2, "Parliament", stmts("Consider the following statements about a joint sitting of the two Houses of Parliament:", [
        "A joint sitting can be called to resolve a deadlock over a Constitution Amendment Bill.",
        "There is no provision for a joint sitting on a Money Bill.",
    ]), TWO, 1,
      "Only statement 2 is correct. Article 108 allows a joint sitting only for ordinary Bills. A Money Bill needs no joint sitting because the Lok Sabha has the final say, and a Constitution Amendment Bill must be passed by each House separately by a special majority."),
    L(1, 2, "CAG", stmts("Consider the following statements about the Comptroller and Auditor General of India:", [
        "The CAG is appointed by the Speaker of the Lok Sabha.",
        "The salary of the CAG is charged on the Consolidated Fund of India.",
    ]), TWO, 1,
      "Only statement 2 is correct. The CAG is appointed by the President under Article 148, and the salary is a charged expenditure, not voted, which protects the office's independence."),
    S(1, 2, "Judicial review", "The basic structure doctrine, which limits Parliament's power to amend the Constitution, was laid down in:",
      ["Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala", "Golaknath v. State of Punjab", "Minerva Mills v. Union of India", "Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India"],
      "In Kesavananda Bharati (1973) a 13-judge bench held that Parliament can amend any part of the Constitution but not its basic structure. Minerva Mills (1980) applied the doctrine, and Golaknath (1967) had earlier barred amending Fundamental Rights."),
    S(1, 1, "Constitutional bodies", "Which of the following is NOT a constitutional body?",
      ["NITI Aayog", "Finance Commission", "Union Public Service Commission", "Election Commission of India"],
      "NITI Aayog was set up in 2015 by an executive resolution of the Union Cabinet. The other three are created by the Constitution (Articles 280, 315 and 324)."),
    L(1, 2, "Attorney General", stmts("Consider the following statements about the Attorney General of India:", [
        "The Attorney General is appointed by the President.",
        "The Attorney General has the right to speak in either House of Parliament.",
        "The Attorney General has the right to vote in Parliament.",
    ], "Which of the statements given above are correct?"), THREE, 0,
      "Statements 1 and 2 are correct. Under Article 88 the Attorney General may speak and take part in the proceedings of either House and its committees, but has no right to vote."),
    S(1, 2, "Emergency", "The words 'armed rebellion' replaced 'internal disturbance' as a ground for a national emergency through the:",
      ["44th Amendment Act, 1978", "42nd Amendment Act, 1976", "38th Amendment Act, 1975", "52nd Amendment Act, 1985"],
      "The 44th Amendment, passed after the 1975 to 1977 Emergency, replaced 'internal disturbance' with 'armed rebellion' in Article 352 and required written advice of the Cabinet before a proclamation."),

    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    L(2, 1, "Citizenship", stmts(CS, [
        "Citizenship at the commencement of the Constitution is dealt with in Part III of the Constitution.",
        "The law on acquiring and losing citizenship was made by Parliament in the Citizenship Act, 1955.",
    ]), TWO, 1,
      "Only statement 2 is correct. Citizenship is in Part II (Articles 5 to 11); Part III contains the Fundamental Rights. Article 11 let Parliament regulate citizenship, which it did through the Citizenship Act, 1955."),
    S(2, 2, "President", "If the President returns an ordinary Bill to Parliament for reconsideration and Parliament passes it again, the President:",
      ["Must give assent to it", "May return it once more", "May refer it to the Supreme Court instead", "May withhold assent for six months"],
      "Under Article 111 the President may return a Bill other than a Money Bill once for reconsideration. If both Houses pass it again, with or without amendments, the President shall not withhold assent. This is often called a suspensive veto."),
    L(2, 3, "Pardoning power", stmts(CS, [
        "The President can pardon a sentence of death.",
        "The Governor of a state can grant a pardon for a sentence passed by a court martial.",
        "The President can pardon a sentence passed by a court martial.",
    ], HOW_ASK), HOW_MANY, 1,
      "Two are correct. Article 72 gives the President power to pardon in all cases where the sentence is death and in all cases of punishment by a court martial. Article 161 gives a Governor no power over court-martial sentences, which concern a Union matter."),
    S(2, 2, "Local government", "Elections to panchayats and municipalities are conducted by the:",
      ["State Election Commission", "Election Commission of India", "District Collector", "State Public Service Commission"],
      "Articles 243K and 243ZA vest the conduct of panchayat and municipal elections in the State Election Commission, appointed by the Governor."),
    L(2, 2, "Supreme Court", stmts(CS, [
        "The Supreme Court has original jurisdiction in disputes between the Government of India and one or more states.",
        "The President may refer a question of law to the Supreme Court for its opinion.",
    ]), TWO, 2,
      "Both are correct. Article 131 gives the Supreme Court original jurisdiction in federal disputes and Article 143 gives it advisory jurisdiction on references from the President."),
    S(2, 1, "GST", "The GST Council, set up under Article 279A, is chaired by:",
      ["The Union Finance Minister", "The Prime Minister", "The Governor of the RBI", "The Chairman of the Finance Commission"],
      "Article 279A, inserted by the 101st Amendment, makes the Union Finance Minister the chairperson; the Union Minister of State for finance and the finance ministers of the states are members."),
    L(2, 2, "Union Territories", stmts("Which of the following Union Territories have a Legislative Assembly?", [
        "Delhi",
        "Puducherry",
        "Chandigarh",
    ], "Select the correct answer using the code given below."), THREE, 0,
      "Delhi and Puducherry have elected legislatures (as does Jammu and Kashmir). Chandigarh is administered directly through an administrator, without a legislature."),
    S(2, 2, "Parliament", "The Speaker of the Lok Sabha resigns by writing to the:",
      ["Deputy Speaker", "President", "Prime Minister", "Vice-President"],
      "Under Article 94 the Speaker resigns by writing to the Deputy Speaker, and the Deputy Speaker by writing to the Speaker."),
    L(2, 1, "Fundamental Rights", stmts(CP, [
        "Article 14 : Abolition of untouchability",
        "Article 21 : Right to constitutional remedies",
        "Article 32 : Uniform civil code for citizens",
    ], PAIRS_ASK), HOW_MANY_PAIRS, 3,
      "None of the pairs is correct. Article 14 is equality before law (untouchability is abolished by Article 17), Article 21 protects life and personal liberty (constitutional remedies are Article 32), and the uniform civil code is the Directive Principle in Article 44."),
    S(2, 2, "Parliament", "In Parliament, Zero Hour refers to:",
      ["Time when members raise urgent matters", "The hour set aside for questions to ministers", "The hour set aside for private members' Bills", "Debate on the President's address"],
      "Zero Hour is when members raise urgent matters of public importance. In the Lok Sabha it usually follows Question Hour at noon. It is an Indian parliamentary practice and is not mentioned in the rules of procedure. The hour for questions to ministers is Question Hour."),
    S(2, 2, "Tribal areas", "Under the Fifth Schedule, an area is declared a Scheduled Area by:",
      ["An order of the President", "A resolution of the state legislature", "The National Commission for Scheduled Tribes", "A notification of the Tribes Advisory Council"],
      "Paragraph 6 of the Fifth Schedule lets the President declare Scheduled Areas by order, after consulting the Governor for any later change. The Tribes Advisory Council advises the Governor on tribal welfare, and the Sixth Schedule's autonomous district councils apply instead in Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram."),
    L(2, 2, "Vice-President", stmts(CS, [
        "The Vice-President is elected by an electoral college of the members of both Houses of Parliament, nominated members included.",
        "Members of the state Legislative Assemblies also vote in the election of the Vice-President.",
    ]), TWO, 0,
      "Only statement 1 is correct. Under Article 66 all members of both Houses, elected and nominated, elect the Vice-President. State legislators vote only in the presidential election."),
    S(2, 1, "Forms of government", "Which of these is a feature of the parliamentary system of government?",
      ["The Cabinet is collectively responsible to the House", "The head of state is also the head of government", "The executive has a fixed term and cannot be removed", "Ministers cannot be members of the legislature"],
      "In a parliamentary system the Council of Ministers is drawn from the legislature and stays in office only while it has the confidence of the lower House (Article 75(3) in India)."),
    S(2, 2, "Centre-state relations", "The President may establish an Inter-State Council under:",
      ["Article 263", "Article 262", "Article 249", "Article 312"],
      "Article 263 provides for an Inter-State Council, first set up in 1990. Article 262 concerns inter-state river water disputes, 249 Parliament's power on state subjects in the national interest, and 312 the all-India services."),
    L(2, 2, "Directive Principles", stmts("Which of the following are Directive Principles of State Policy?", [
        "Equal pay for equal work for both men and women",
        "Organisation of village panchayats",
        "Protection of monuments of national importance",
    ], "Select the correct answer using the code given below."), THREE, 3,
      "All three. They are in Articles 39(d), 40 and 49 respectively, all in Part IV."),
    S(2, 2, "Financial committees", "The members of the Public Accounts Committee are drawn from:",
      ["15 Lok Sabha and 7 Rajya Sabha members", "The Lok Sabha only, 30 members in all", "The Rajya Sabha only, 15 members in all", "The Union Cabinet and the CAG's office"],
      "The PAC has 22 members, 15 from the Lok Sabha and 7 from the Rajya Sabha, and examines the CAG's audit reports. The Estimates Committee is drawn from the Lok Sabha only."),

    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 1, "Centre-state relations", "Under the Indian Constitution, the residuary power to make laws on matters not in any of the three lists belongs to:",
      ["Parliament", "The state legislatures", "The President", "The Supreme Court"],
      "Article 248 and Entry 97 of the Union List give Parliament the residuary power, a feature taken from Canada."),
    L(0, 2, "New states", stmts("Consider the following statements about forming a new state under Article 3:", [
        "A Bill to form a new state can be introduced in Parliament only on the recommendation of the President.",
        "Parliament is bound to accept the views of the state legislature concerned.",
    ]), TWO, 0,
      "Only statement 1 is correct. The President refers the Bill to the affected state legislature for its views within a set time, but Parliament is not bound by those views."),
]
